        {
          "path": "_m/6/2/7/q/chiloe-national-park-wk/boardwalk-at-parque-nacional-c.jpg",
          "filename": "Boardwalk at Parque Nacional Chiloé (3162706127).jpg",
          "caption": "The \"Educational Trail\" outside Parque Nacional Chiloé, a spectacular half-kilometer boardwalk through \"Tepual\", a sort of half-rainforest, half-sphagnum bog typical of lowlands along the west coast of Chiloé Island.  \n\nUnfortunately, in what seems to be keeping with the spirit of almost all the sadly unmaintained parks throughout Chile, this park has only one trail left -- this one.  All the others shown on various brochures and even on the official CONAF website don't appear to exist any longer.  You can still get to Cole Cole (a spectacularly beautiful walk and beach with remnants of a refugio still open to the public), because this \"trail\" is kept open by locals who live in the area (ask locals for directions) -- bear in mind Cole Cole isn't actually in the park!  Unfortunately everything inside the park has fallen completely into disrepair.  You can still see where the trail starts, for example, that leads to the next cove, but it is so overgrown by bamboo that I found it easier to go off trail... which is to say, there was essentially no trail anymore.",
          "alt": "The \"Educational Trail\" outside Parque Nacional Chiloé, a spectacular half-kilometer boardwalk through \"Tepual\", a sort of half-rainforest, half-sphagnum bog typical of lowlands along the west coast of Chiloé Island.  \n\nUnfortunately, in what seems to be keeping with the spirit of almost all the sadly unmaintained parks throughout Chile, this park has only one trail left -- this one.  All the others shown on various brochures and even on the official CONAF website don't appear to exist any longer.  You can still get to Cole Cole (a spectacularly beautiful walk and beach with remnants of a refugio still open to the public), because this \"trail\" is kept open by locals who live in the area (ask locals for directions) -- bear in mind Cole Cole isn't actually in the park!  Unfortunately everything inside the park has fallen completely into disrepair.  You can still see where the trail starts, for example, that leads to the next cove, but it is so overgrown by bamboo that I found it easier to go off trail... which is to say, there was essentially no trail anymore.",
          "width": 2432,
          "height": 3648,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Jason Hollinger",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boardwalk_at_Parque_Nacional_Chilo%C3%A9_(3162706127).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "chiloe-national-park-wk",
          "sourceGeohash4": "627q"
